title: Example - Markdown escape sequences
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki;vocab=Markdown

A backslash before an ASCII punctuation character renders the character literally — the backslash is consumed, the punctuation is emitted as text. CommonMark Section 6.1. Lets authors write `*literal asterisks*` without triggering italic, `` ` `` without a code span, `[` without a link, etc.

## Common cases

Live: \*not italic\* and \_not underscore\_ and \`not code\`

Source:

```
\*not italic\* and \_not underscore\_ and \`not code\`
```

Each backslash + punctuation pair renders as just the punctuation. The italic / underscore / code-span markers never see their open characters because the backslash escape consumes them first.

## Bracket escapes

Live: \[not a link\] and \!\[not an image\](url)

Source:

```
\[not a link\] and \!\[not an image\](url)
```

The brackets and bang stay literal — the LINK and IMAGE markers don't fire.

## Backslash itself

Live: a \\ literal backslash

Source:

```
a \\ literal backslash
```

`\\` → `\` (backslash is itself in the escapable set).

## Non-punctuation: backslash stays

Live: \A \word \123 — backslashes stay literal when the next char isn't ASCII punctuation.

Source:

```
\A \word \123 — backslashes stay literal when the next char isn't ASCII punctuation.
```

CommonMark spec: `\A`, `\word`, `\1` render as `\A`, `\word`, `\1` (backslash NOT consumed).

## Inside code spans and code blocks

Backslash escapes don't fire inside `body-raw` content. A code span keeps backslashes verbatim:

Live: `\*literal in code\*`

Source:

````
`\*literal in code\*`
````

Same inside fenced code blocks — body is captured raw, no inline parsing, so backslashes survive.

## The full escapable set

CommonMark allows the backslash to escape these 30 ASCII punctuation chars:

```
! " # $ % & ' ( ) * + , - . / : ; < = > ? @ [ \ ] ^ _ ` { | } ~
```

Any other character following a `\` leaves both chars literal.

